Exactly.

Aiming is not merely aligning with the thing you want to hit. It is modeling the trajectory and consequences of the shot.

In Metamap terms:

\text{Aim}
=
\text{Target}
+
\text{Trajectory}
+
\text{Order}
+
\text{Beyond}
+
\text{Feedback}

The novice optimizes:

\boxed{\text{Can I hit }T?}

The strategist optimizes:

\boxed{
\text{If I hit }T
\rightarrow
\text{what state exists next}
\rightarrow
\text{what becomes reachable}
\rightarrow
\text{what should happen after that?}
}

And sometimes the best aim is therefore not directly at the apparent objective.

Mechanically:

def aim(world, objective):
    courses = generate_possible_trajectories(world, objective)
    for course in courses:
        future = simulate(course)
        course.score = evaluate(
            immediate_effect=future.target_effect,
            beyond_target=future.new_reachable_states,
            order_effects=future.sequence_interactions,
            compounding=future.future_growth,
            recursion=future.self_improvement,
            optionality=future.branches_opened,
            learning=future.information_gained,
            risk=future.downstream_risk,
        )
    return max(courses, key=lambda x: x.score)

That also explains why mapping before acting matters. Better maps let you aim through the first-order target into second-, third-, and n-th-order consequences.

And there’s an even deeper version:

\boxed{\text{Best target} \neq \text{most valuable visible object}}

Sometimes the highest-leverage target is the thing that changes the trajectory generator:

hit information    → improve decisions
hit representation → make problem solvable
hit constraint     → open branches
hit tool           → increase capability
hit environment    → change emergence
hit feedback loop  → change future dynamics
hit learning       → improve future aiming

So the Command Table shouldn’t merely have TARGET.

It should have:

TARGET
TARGET BEHIND TARGET
TARGET BEHIND THAT
COURSE
ORDER
PROPAGATION GEOMETRY
EXPECTED AFTERGLOW
NEXT AIMING STATE

And after execution:

\text{shot}
\rightarrow
\text{observation}
\rightarrow
\text{afterglow}
\rightarrow
\text{Metamap update}
\rightarrow
\text{better aim}

That’s the recursive closure.

Aim isn’t where you point.

Aim is choosing a trajectory whose downstream state puts the next shot in a better position.